docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:37:| P24 | Minimum window 1180×720 (DESIGN-V2 §4, tokens.md) | The host keeps `min_inner_size(900, 600)` until C34 because C01-N09 (re-run cumulatively by C24-N06) pins the legacy minimum; the v2 shell is laid out to stay usable without horizontal scroll at 900×600 (rail collapsed to icons below 1280 CSS px) and C28-N07 asserts it at that size. C34 raises the minimum together with the legacy-script retirement | Legacy native acceptance must keep passing while both shells exist (D058 §5) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:38:| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: "secrets enter only through a host secret sheet" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:39:| P26 | `verifyTenantSetup` as one core intent streaming `VerifyStep`s (wizard.md) | Implemented as specified: `verifyTenantSetup{context}` runs in the core over the draft, admits the reads it needs through the management pipeline (management verify; the chosen Gateway workspace's prerequisites, or only the listing when none is chosen, reported as incomplete coverage; Red Team; model/skill) and settles each line from the result; AIS/AES are listed as not available and never read; the lines, `done`, `failed`, `blocking` and `stale` stream through `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify`. The scan key has no admitted read: its line is `stored`, never `pass`. Only the scan key (first run) blocks; an optional service's failure is carried as a warning the operator may continue over | Attempt-01 composed the reads in the presentation; attempt-02 read only the workspace listing and blocked on every failure — both rejected |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:40:| P27 | Add-tenant Cancel writes nothing except an already-vaulted credential | The wizard works on a core-owned draft (`beginTenantSetup`) with its own context and its own Gateway listing and choice; credentials attach to the draft; nothing is listed as a tenant (a draft id is refused by `selectTenant`/`deleteTenant`), the active tenant and its Gateway choice are untouched until `completeTenantSetup` commits the draft in one step and switches once. Every change to the draft's credentials or profile retires its context (and the binding, reads and listing under it). Cancel (`discardTenantSetup`) drops the draft, retires the binding and every read under its context and workspace reference, and queues its Keychain records for cleanup; the sheet closes only once the core admits it (a creation still pending or unconfirmed is awaited and whatever it produced is discarded). A relaunch, or a tenant committed outside the wizard during first run, does the same | Stronger than the spec's leftover credential: no orphan secrets and no cached authority surviving a replaced credential |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:41:| P28 | Gateway workspace key entered on the wizard tenant step | Implemented: an optional section reads the workspaces through the management client (`management` gateway inventory on the draft context), `selectGatewayWorkspace` chooses one, and `configureInferenceCredential` saves the endpoint and key for it | The admitted sequence (list → select → key) runs on the draft like on a tenant |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:46:| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a failed refresh keeps the earlier read labelled as older | No second read pipeline, and Home never claims coverage a read did not establish (I4): revised after the C30 attempt-01 review, which found selected Red Team and model/skill products left unread and a workspace-less Gateway announced as complete |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:47:| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:49:| P36 | Completing "Add a tenant" switches to the new tenant | `completeTenantSetup{resolution?}` goes through the same guard as `selectTenant`: a pending Harness decision refuses it with `switchBlocked{to: <draft id>, completion: true}`, nothing is committed or retired, the draft stays, and the wizard renders the one guard dialog and re-sends the completion with the resolution | Wizard completion is listed among the entry points of the single guarded switch path (shell.md, I1); the attempt-01 review found it bypassing the guard |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:50:| P37 | Scopes are per tenant and a switch round trip shows the cached state | Each opened tenant owns its read record in the core (its last overview, the other products' outcomes, generation, recheck, snooze receipt) for the session; a switch away and back shows it again without a new read. Per-operation bookkeeping is separate from the cached data: each read part is tracked with the context it was admitted under, "reading" means a part under the current context is open, a part under a retired context is withdrawn when it lands (never recorded, never counted), so a read held open across a switch round trip can never leave the tenant reading. The presentation's own transient state follows the tenant too: the shell store carries the shown scope (set from each snapshot before it renders); a toast for a tenant's item or read and an unconfirmed delivery bound to a context are refused at insertion when that scope is no longer shown (a refusal or lost delivery arriving after a switch), retired on leaving it (however long Reduce Motion would keep them) and filtered at render; a contextual arrival belongs to the tenant it was followed in | I2 names the tenant, not the context, as the owner of its cached scopes; the attempt-01 review found the overview bound to the context and lost across A → B → A |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:52:| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:58:| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:59:| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; "Open the saved transcript" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:60:| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:61:| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:62:| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:63:| P50 | A composer draft per conversation | One composer per tenant and workspace (by the stable workspace ID): its draft survives navigation, tenant round trips and the retirement of the conversation it was typed for. A message the core did not take is kept under "Not sent"; one whose delivery was not confirmed under "Not confirmed", settled only by an operator entry its own conversation added after it (one whose entry ID it did not show when it was sent: the core numbers a conversation's entries and never reuses an ID, while it drops the oldest entries past 200 entries or 1 MiB, so neither a count nor a position stays comparable) that no admitted send claims (a competing send's claim only holds its entry back while the send is in flight, is used up once the send is admitted, and is released if the send is refused or not confirmed); each entry settles one record, once. Nothing is sent by itself | A conversation's draft must stay recoverable after the core retires the conversation (C32 reviews) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:64:| P51 | A new investigation prefilled with the ended conversation's opening question | A contextual arrival stays pending, bound to its tenant and workspace, until the conversation or the history resolves it ("Finding that investigation…" while the saved conversations are listed); listed without it, or not listable, the page says so; navigation, a new arrival, another workspace or a restart cancels it. The opening question is the conversation's opening message, whole, kept by the core apart from its title (a one-line, shortened label for lists): `ConversationView.opening` and `OpenedConversationView.opening`, captured when the first message (`u-1`) enters the transcript, saved with the record, restored with it and kept after the transcript drops its oldest entries. An archived conversation's record is read for it when the restart is asked for (nothing is filled in meanwhile); that read settles by the core's receipt for the record (`HarnessHistoryView.lastOpen`: id, admission order, failure), newer than any open settled before it was asked for, never by a busy edge or a changed error. When it is not known (a record saved before C32 whose transcript no longer holds its first message) or the record cannot be read, the page says so and never fills in the title instead; a draft in the box is kept under Not sent and nothing is sent | The title is shortened (72 or 80 characters, one line) and lost the rest of a long or multi-line question; the attempt-12 review found the restart prefilling it |
